{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkdS3w1NkyN2397F3T3A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`contract/claim.compact` is written and compiles cleanly with `compact compile`. I didn't run it beyond compiling.\n\n- **Public key:** `publicKey(sk)` is `persistentHash([pad(32, \"claim:pk:\"), sk])`. It's an exported pure circuit, so the app can use it directly.\n- **Claim record:** `claim()` hashes the secret key with a different domain tag, `\"claim:nullifier:\"`. It asserts that this nullifier isn't already in the `claimed` set, then inserts it and increments `claims`. A second claim with the same key fails with \"Already claimed\".\n- **Privacy:** the public ledger holds only the nullifier, never the public key. The two hashes use different domain tags, so a public key can't be turned into its nullifier. Someone who sees a public key can't check whether it has claimed.\n- **Witnesses:** `secretKey()` is the only one.\n\nI added two things you didn't ask for. `claimed` is an exported ledger `Set<Bytes<32>>` of nullifiers, and the non-exported helper circuit `nullifier()` computes them.\n\nThe nullifier is deliberately disclosed with `disclose()`. That's safe because it's a one-way hash that can't be linked to the public key."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":162,"cache_read_input_tokens":21823,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":162},"output_tokens":416,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c046c6a-03c2-4a95-8762-a7362e2490fb","uuid":"7104fa7a-6258-4244-a2dc-c665debef758","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:13:14.883Z","request_id":"req_011CgkdS3ftKctTqNxa6PP3S"}
